TaraSec Hotspot
===============

New installations should use the automatic hotspot installer at the repository root:

    sudo bash tara_hotspot.sh

The installer is designed to require no questions on a normal machine. It automatically:

- detects the interface carrying the IPv4 default route and uses it as WAN;
- finds an unused Wi-Fi interface whose driver supports AP mode;
- unblocks software-blocked Wi-Fi;
- chooses a private /24 that does not conflict with existing routes or addresses;
- installs and configures hostapd and dnsmasq without replacing the machine's global dnsmasq configuration;
- enables IPv4 forwarding using a dedicated sysctl drop-in;
- creates TaraSec-specific firewall/NAT chains without changing the host's global FORWARD policy;
- installs openNDS when it is available from the system package repositories;
- preserves an existing NetBird connection and does not require NetBird enrollment for local hotspot operation;
- creates backups before changing TaraSec-owned configuration;
- validates hostapd/dnsmasq configuration and performs a basic health check;
- can be rerun safely.

Default hotspot
---------------

SSID: TaraSec
Wi-Fi security: open (intended for captive-portal access)
Channel: 6
DHCP clients: .50 through .200 on the automatically selected /24

The installer stores its detected configuration in:

    /etc/tarasec/hotspot.conf

Advanced overrides
------------------

Most users should not need these. They are useful for unusual hardware or automated deployment:

    TARASEC_HOTSPOT_IF=wlan1
    TARASEC_WAN_IF=eth0
    TARASEC_SSID=TaraSec
    TARASEC_WIFI_PASSWORD='optional-wpa2-password'
    TARASEC_HOTSPOT_CIDR=192.168.50.0/24
    TARASEC_COUNTRY=NO
    TARASEC_CHANNEL=6
    TARASEC_INSTALL_OPENNDS=auto   # auto, yes, or no
    TARASEC_NETBIRD_SETUP_KEY=...
    TARASEC_NETBIRD_MGMT_URL=https://netbird.taransvar.no
    TARASEC_NONINTERACTIVE=1

If several unused AP-capable Wi-Fi adapters are present, the installer asks which one to use. In non-interactive mode it stops instead, so automation never guesses between equally plausible radios.

Existing/legacy hotspot code
----------------------------

The files under hotspot/perl and older FreeRADIUS-based setup routines are retained for compatibility and migration work. They are not the recommended installation path for a new TaraSec hotspot. In particular, do not follow older notes that require manual RADIUS addresses, per-user paths, or hand-edited cron entries.

Troubleshooting
---------------

Useful commands:

    cat /etc/tarasec/hotspot.conf
    ip -br addr
    ip route
    iw dev
    systemctl status hostapd dnsmasq tarasec-hotspot-interface tarasec-hotspot-firewall --no-pager
    journalctl -u hostapd -u dnsmasq -n 100 --no-pager
    iptables -S TARASEC-HOTSPOT-FWD
    iptables -t nat -S TARASEC-HOTSPOT-NAT

If the installer cannot identify an unused AP-capable Wi-Fi interface, adding a supported USB Wi-Fi adapter is usually enough; the next run will detect it automatically.
